# Old build URLs -> the current build.
# Beta was the published app link for the whole v0.9.9 run, Delta was live briefly,
# and Echo was the published link until v1.1. All are kept working for anyone who
# bookmarked them directly.
#
# 302 (not 301) on purpose: these are "whatever is current" aliases, and a 301
# would be cached permanently by browsers, making the next build swap unfixable
# for anyone who had followed the old link.
#
# RETIRING A BUILD TAKES TWO STEPS, and a rule alone is not enough:
#   1. Move its .html out of the repo root, into versions/<ver>/.
#   2. Point its URL here.
# Netlify serves a matching file in preference to a rule in this file, so while the
# build sits in the root its rule never fires -- silently. That is not theoretical:
# Echo's rule was added and did nothing, because Echo's file was still in the root,
# and anyone with the Echo bookmark carried on being served a build that loads its
# export libraries from cdnjs and accepts .wma. Both are fixed in v1.1, and both
# were invisible from this file alone. Beta and Delta worked only because their
# files had already been deleted.
#
# The alternative is to force a rule with a trailing "!" (302!), which beats the
# file. Archiving is preferred: it leaves no build sitting in the root that is
# served at no URL, and versions/ is where old builds already live.
#
# v1.0.0 has no rule on purpose. It was built and deployed but never linked from the
# landing page, so nobody has it bookmarked. Its file stays in the root and stays
# reachable as a snapshot of the first self-contained build.

# The old Netlify address -> the real domain.
# theatre-cue-player.netlify.app and theatrecueplayer.app are the same Netlify site,
# and Netlify does NOT redirect one to the other on its own -- it just serves both,
# so the app was reachable at two addresses. This rule sends every netlify.app URL
# to the same path on theatrecueplayer.app.
#
# The trailing "!" is required: every path here (/, /theatrecueplayer_v1.1, ...) has
# a real file behind it, and without the "!" the file wins and the rule never fires
# -- the same silent failure described above.
#
# 301 (not 302) on purpose: unlike the build aliases, this one is genuinely permanent.
# The domain is not going to change back. Browsers will cache it, which is the point.

https://theatre-cue-player.netlify.app/*  https://theatrecueplayer.app/:splat  301!

/theatrecueplayer_v0.9.9_beta       /theatrecueplayer_v1.1   302
/theatrecueplayer_v0.9.9_delta      /theatrecueplayer_v1.1   302
/theatrecueplayer_v0.9.9_echo       /theatrecueplayer_v1.1   302
/TheatreCuePlayer_v0.9.9_Beta.html  /theatrecueplayer_v1.1   302
/TheatreCuePlayer_v0.9.9_Delta.html /theatrecueplayer_v1.1   302
/TheatreCuePlayer_v0.9.9_Echo.html  /theatrecueplayer_v1.1   302
